The kernel's network stack will call the ndo_stop method when the network interface is stopped. This can occur in several scenarios:

	1) Interface Down (ifconfig down or ip link set <interface> down):

		>When the user runs the command ifconfig <interface> down or ip link set <interface> down, the interface is administratively brought
		 down, and the kernel calls the ndo_stop method for the device.

		>In this case, the interface is disabled, and the cpsw_ndo_stop function is called to cleanly shut down the interface and release its
		 resources.

	2) Shutdown of the Network Interface During Power Management:

		>If the system is going into a low-power state or shutting down, the kernel might stop all network interfaces before suspending or
		 shutting down the system.

		>In this case, the ndo_stop method would be called to stop the CPSW device and perform cleanup before the device is powered down.
		
		
Summary of What the Function Does:

The cpsw_ndo_stop function is invoked when the CPSW device (the Ethernet interface) is being stopped, typically as part of shutting down or bringing the network interface down.

The function performs several tasks:

	It disables network features like NAPI, interrupts, and DMA if necessary.

	It stops transmission queues and disables carrier detection.

	It ensures that the device's resources are cleaned up and the device is properly powered down.

	It iterates over slave interfaces (Ethernet ports) and stops them as well.

	It also decrements the usage count and performs any necessary cleanup steps related to device power management.
	
	
	
	
In Detail Function Breakdown:

1. Retrieve Driver Private Data:
-----------------------------------
```
	   struct cpsw_priv *priv = netdev_priv(ndev);
	   struct cpsw_common *cpsw = priv->cpsw;
	
````
	   The priv variable holds the private data for the network device (ndev),
	   which includes the CPSW-specific data structure (cpsw_common).
	   
	   priv->cpsw points to the common CPSW data structure, which is shared by 
	   different components of the CPSW driver.

2. Log the Shutdown:
----------------------
```
   cpsw_info(priv, ifdown, "shutting down cpsw device\n");
```

   The function logs a message that indicates the CPSW device is being shut down
   using cpsw_info. This is typically for debugging or informational purposes.

3. Remove Multicast Address Synchronization:
--------------------------------------------
```
   __hw_addr_ref_unsync_dev(&ndev->mc, ndev, cpsw_purge_all_mc);
```

   This function call unsynchronizes the multicast address list (ndev->mc) from
   the device, ensuring that the device will not be referencing stale multicast
   addresses when it is stopped.
   
   The cpsw_purge_all_mc function will be called to remove any multicast address
   references associated with the device.

4. Stop All Transmit Queues:
-------------------------------
```
   netif_tx_stop_all_queues(priv->ndev);
```

   This stops all transmit queues of the network device. It ensures that no packets
   are being transmitted while the device is being stopped.

5. Turn Off Carrier Detection:
--------------------------------
```
   netif_carrier_off(priv->ndev);
```

   This function indicates that the network device has lost carrier (i.e., the
   physical link is down). It marks the device as "disconnected" from the network.

6. Conditionally Disable Features Based on Usage Count:
---------------------------------------------------------
```
   if (cpsw->usage_count <= 1) {
       napi_disable(&cpsw->napi_rx);
       napi_disable(&cpsw->napi_tx);
       cpts_unregister(cpsw->cpts);
       cpsw_intr_disable(cpsw);
       cpdma_ctlr_stop(cpsw->dma);
       cpsw_ale_stop(cpsw->ale);
       cpsw_destroy_xdp_rxqs(cpsw);
   }
```

   cpsw->usage_count <= 1 checks if there is only one user of the CPSW device
   (meaning no other references are using it). If true, the following steps occur:
   
   - napi_disable(&cpsw->napi_rx): Disables the NAPI (New API) for RX (receive)
     processing. NAPI is used for efficient interrupt handling and polling in Linux
     networking.
   
   - napi_disable(&cpsw->napi_tx): Disables NAPI for TX (transmit) processing.

   - cpts_unregister(cpsw->cpts): Unregisters the CPTS (Cascaded Timestamping)
     unit. This unit is responsible for handling hardware timestamps for transmitted
     and received packets.
   
   - cpsw_intr_disable(cpsw): Disables interrupts in the CPSW module.

   - cpdma_ctlr_stop(cpsw->dma): Stops the CPDMA (Cascaded Peripheral DMA) controller,
     which handles DMA-based packet transmission and reception.
   
   - cpsw_ale_stop(cpsw->ale): Stops the ALE (Address Lookup Engine), which is
     responsible for managing MAC address lookups in the switch.
   
   - cpsw_destroy_xdp_rxqs(cpsw): Destroys any XDP (eXpress Data Path) receive queues.
     XDP is a high-performance mechanism for packet processing in the Linux kernel.

7. Stop Each Slave (Ethernet Port):
-------------------------------------
```
   for_each_slave(priv, cpsw_slave_stop, cpsw);
```

   Iterates through each slave (physical Ethernet port) managed by the CPSW and
   calls cpsw_slave_stop on each one. This effectively stops each individual slave port.

8. Check and Handle Resplitting:
-----------------------------------
``
   if (cpsw_need_resplit(cpsw))
       cpsw_split_res(cpsw);
```

   This checks if the CPSW device needs to resplit the traffic across multiple
   queues or devices. If true, cpsw_split_res is called to handle the resplitting.

9. Decrement Usage Count:
--------------------------
```
   cpsw->usage_count--;
```

   The usage_count is decremented, which keeps track of how many times the CPSW
   device is being used. This count is important for managing the lifecycle of the
   device and freeing resources when no longer needed.

10. Release Power Management Resources:
----------------------------------------
```
    pm_runtime_put_sync(cpsw->dev);
```
    This call reduces the reference count for the device's runtime power management.
    If the reference count reaches zero, the device will be powered down. The
    pm_runtime_put_sync ensures that power management operations are synchronized.

11. Return:
-------------

    return 0;


	
	
